\@writefile{tdo}{\contentsline {todo}{\fcolorbox {black}{pink}{\textcolor {pink}{o}}\ This comes from matching $F = ma$ on the boundary (a dynamic boundary condition), and hence $\mathbf  {T}_g\cdot \hat  {\underline  {n}} = \mathbf  {T}_l\cdot \hat  {\underline  {n}}$. As the liquid is inviscid, $\mathbf  {T}_l = -p\mathbf  {I}$, and so we have $p=p$. Also, for this we need high Weber number, such that capillary pressure jumps across the interface are negligible at leading order.}{5}{section*.16}\protected@file@percent }
