\@writefile{tdo}{\contentsline {todo}{\fcolorbox {black}{green}{\textcolor {green}{o}}\ okidoki, so here's what I'm thinking: I think it's all well and good to state all the singularity analysis and stuff like that, but it seems to complicated to do this in code that I thought it would be so much easier to use the anti-derivative version of \eqref  {BoundaryIntegralAntiderivative}, such that we approximate $f(r) = \frac  {\partial p_l(r,0)}{\partial r}\ r$ as locally constant? RESEARCH THAT $\partial _rp_l(r,0)$ IS??? Or, better yet, we account for the linear / quadratic deviation in f(r) by restoring the linear $-\frac  {\ln 2}{\pi r'}f'(r')\ dr'$ term in the integral? This linear addition makes things absolutely identical to the Cauchy PV addition and subtraction, as we are specifically adding in $\intop \ilimits@ _{r_i - \Delta r/2}^{r_i + \Delta r/2} \big  [f'(r_i)(r' - r_i)\big  ]\left [\frac  {1}{\pi r_i(r_i - r')}\right ] dr' = -\frac  {f'(r_i)}{\pi r_i}\Delta r$, and using the evenness of the integral to cancel out the rest? Note for future me - do both! Write locally constant behaviour, then locally affine, and compare the full convergence in a log-log graph to show that this locally linear bit is the same. May also be worth comparing this to the full Cauchy PV method in the same suite (!!!), unless you can convince yourself that they are indeed the exact same thing - I don't quite get it yet! :O}{11}{section*.52}\protected@file@percent }
\pgfsyspdfmark {pgfid205}{19562753}{9893961}
\@writefile{tdo}{\contentsline {todo}{\fcolorbox {black}{green}{\textcolor {green}{o}}\ Hello past Alfie, it's Alfie from 15 minutes after you wrote this, it's very easy lmao. Basically, FVM solvers have error $O(\Delta r^2)$ in discretising the pressure function into points (seen with derivative functions), so we need to make sure that the integrand has error no worse than $O(\Delta r^2)$? In other words, even if we add Taylor quadratic terms to the approximation, we approximate with $O(\Delta r^3)$, which is lost completely to the FVM solver! Basically, as long as the error is no worse than $O(\Delta r^2)$, we are as accurate as we can be! (to leading order) But EVERYTHING needs to be of this order! Also elliptical integrals are smooth so we're fine there too!}{11}{section*.53}\protected@file@percent }
\pgfsyspdfmark {pgfid213}{0}{0}
\pgfsyspdfmark {pgfid206}{19562753}{47678229}
\@writefile{tdo}{\contentsline {todo}{\fcolorbox {black}{pink}{\textcolor {pink}{o}}\ Figure out what adding PV means analytically, then how we can reduce this: evaluate E(1), as this is where the singularity is (cauchy pole, logarithmic singularity?) Do this analutiically, subtract dp/dr (CITE Inertial and viscous dynamics of jump-to-contact between fluid drops under van der Waals attraction): find a known value that works, evaluate the integral to get something known: " We note that a constant pressure satisfies both Laplace’s equation and the far-field and regularity conditions required in the derivation of (A3). We can thus set dp/dr = 1 in (A3) to obtain the identity...".}{12}{section*.54}\protected@file@percent }
\pgfsyspdfmark {pgfid208}{19562753}{41234098}
\@writefile{tdo}{\contentsline {todo}{\fcolorbox {black}{pink}{\textcolor {pink}{o}}\ In the numerical method, the elliptical integrals $K(m)$ and $E(m)$ are well-approximated by $P(\xi )-Q(\xi )\ln \xi $ and $R(\xi )-S(\xi )\ln \xi $ respectively, where $P,Q,R,S$ are tabulated polynomials, and $\chi = 1 - m^2$ (CITE BOUNDARY INTEGRAL METHODS APPLIED TO CAVITATION BUBBLE DVNAMICS).}{12}{section*.55}\protected@file@percent }
\pgfsyspdfmark {pgfid209}{19562753}{36386097}
\@writefile{tdo}{\contentsline {todo}{\fcolorbox {black}{pink}{\textcolor {pink}{o}}\ "CITE BOUNDARY INTEGRAL METHODS APPLIED TO CAVITATION BUBBLE DVNAMICS section 2.6": something something isolate and subtract logarithmic singularities across boundary elements before applying numerical quadrature, citing Stroud and Secrest (1966) (Gaussian Quadrature Formulas). The particular integral has an explicit singularity of log type which can be integrated using the quadrature scheme tabulated by Stroud and Secrest $\intop \ilimits@ _0^1 \ln (1/x)f(x)\ dx$".}{12}{section*.56}\protected@file@percent }
