\@writefile{tdo}{\contentsline {todo}{\fcolorbox {black}{green}{\textcolor {green}{o}}\ To do it all yourself: for the case of $i = j$ we split the integral at the singularity $r_i$, grade each side by cutting into panels of width half the previous, slowly converging towards the singularity, then use \texttt  {np.polynomial.legendre.leggauss(20)} for each of these! Finally, we add in the analytic singularity $\mathcal  {K} \approx \frac  {1}{\pi r_i} \ln \frac  {8r_i}{|\epsilon }$, which gives $\intop \ilimits@ _0^a\frac  {1}{\pi r_i}\ln \frac  {8r_i}{\epsilon }\,d\epsilon =\frac  {a}{\pi r_i}\left (\ln \frac  {8r_i}{a}+1\right )$, neglecting the term afterwards which is $\mathcal  {O}(a^2/r_i^2) \sim 10^{-24}$.}{15}{section*.62}\protected@file@percent }
\pgfsyspdfmark {pgfid233}{0}{0}
