\@writefile{tdo}{\contentsline {todo}{\fcolorbox {black}{orange}{\textcolor {orange}{o}}\ Doesn't this assumption break down for the innermost cell(s)?? Do we not care just because we're in a very local region? However, the r in f(r) also scales the solution down - near r = 0 we are very close to quadratic and so f is $\mathcal  {O}(1)$ in this small region, not $\mathcal  {O}(r)$! Small note though that we lose 3.3\% accuracy in this method vs the full exact integral - is this bad?? Apparently we can calculate $\mathbf  {K}^{(1),\text  {exact}}_{11} = \left (\frac  {2K(8/9)}{3\pi } + 1\right ) - \frac  {4G}{\pi } - \frac  {2}{\pi }\intop \ilimits@ _{1/2}^1 \frac  {K(v^2)}{v}\,dv$? But then why not just do this always?! Maybe begin with the standard method and then inject this in to see what happens? The fix here is to consider dp/dr as piecewise linear, and then multiply by r at the end! This decreases the error by like a 10th!}{16}{section*.63}\protected@file@percent }
